{# ═══ FAMILY HERO ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════
   The product-led alternative to pagehead.njk, for a family whose
   packaging is strong enough to lead with. Opt-in: a family only gets
   this if its record in families.json carries a `hero` object, exactly
   the way a product opts into a background photo with `heroBackground`.
   Every family without one renders pagehead.njk unchanged.

   Same contract as pagehead.njk in the one way that matters: the nav
   carries margin-bottom:-72px and overlays whatever sits under it, so
   this has to be a crimson field of its own, and .fhero-grid has to open
   with 72px of padding or the nav lands on the headline.

   Expects `family.hero`: { heading, rail[], deck, note, actions[], packs[] }
   plus the same `ph.crumbs` the page head uses. ═══════════════════ #}
<section class="fhero">
  <div class="fhero-topo" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 1200 900"><use href="#c-ridge"/></svg></div>
  <div class="shell fhero-grid">

    <div class="fhero-copy">
      {%- if ph.crumbs %}
      <nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <ol>
          <li><a href="/">Home</a></li>
          {%- for c in ph.crumbs %}
          <li>
            {%- if c.url %}<a href="{{ c.url }}">{{ c.label }}</a>
            {%- else %}<span aria-current="page">{{ c.label }}</span>{% endif %}
          </li>
          {%- endfor %}
        </ol>
      </nav>
      {%- endif %}

      <h1 class="t-display fhero-h1 enter">{{ family.hero.heading | safe }}</h1>

      {#- The rail is the reference's own device: the range stated as a
          measured list rather than described in a sentence. Set in the
          display face, which is the one place on the site it does a job
          other than a headline — it is what makes the line read as
          stamped on the pack rather than as a caption under it. #}
      {%- if family.hero.rail %}
      <p class="fhero-rail enter" data-d="1">
        {%- for r in family.hero.rail %}<span>{{ r }}</span>{% endfor -%}
      </p>
      {%- endif %}

      {%- if family.hero.deck %}
      <p class="fhero-deck enter" data-d="1">{{ family.hero.deck }}</p>
      {%- endif %}

      {%- if family.hero.actions %}
      <div class="fhero-actions enter" data-d="2">
        {%- for a in family.hero.actions %}
        <a class="btn {{ a.style }}" href="{{ a.url }}">{{ a.label }}</a>
        {%- endfor %}
      </div>
      {%- endif %}

      {%- if family.hero.note %}
      <p class="fhero-note enter" data-d="3">{{ family.hero.note }}</p>
      {%- endif %}
    </div>

    {#- The packs are real photography, cut out by gen-gummies-shots.mjs.
        Staggered rather than side by side, for the same reason the
        homepage hero staggers its cans: two objects at one depth read as
        a product grid, and the point here is a group portrait. #}
    <div class="fhero-packs">
      <span class="pack-pool" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      <span class="pack-floor" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      {#- The slot carries the position, the img carries the animation, and
          they MUST stay separate elements — this is not decoration.
          `.enter` runs @keyframes rise, which ends on `transform:none` and
          is declared `forwards`, so an animation-filled transform beats
          any transform set on the same element in the cascade. Put both on
          one element and the packs stack dead centre on top of each other
          with no stagger at all. The homepage cans are built the same way
          (.can-slot positions, .can.enter animates) for the same reason.

          No width/height attributes: the two cutouts have different aspect
          ratios and both are sized off the container's height, so there is
          no layout shift for an intrinsic ratio to prevent. #}
      {%- for p in family.hero.packs %}
      <span class="pack-slot {{ p.slot }}">
        <img class="pack enter" data-d="{{ loop.index }}"
             src="{{ p.src }}" alt="{{ p.alt }}" fetchpriority="high">
      </span>
      {%- endfor %}
    </div>

  </div>
</section>
